{"id":3722,"date":"2021-09-16T14:02:39","date_gmt":"2021-09-16T21:02:39","guid":{"rendered":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/?post_type=lesson&#038;p=3722"},"modified":"2023-08-21T08:35:49","modified_gmt":"2023-08-21T15:35:49","slug":"2-6-user-input","status":"publish","type":"lesson","link":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/2-6-user-input\/","title":{"rendered":"2.6: User Input"},"content":{"rendered":"<div class=\"wpb-content-wrapper\"><p>[vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22147a39686\" name=\"6a22147a39686\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 1<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Goals and Steps to Success<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Goals:<\/p>\n<p>By the end of this lesson, I will be able to:<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Identify the importance of input in programs<\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Create and run programs that include user interaction<\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Store and modify user inputs<\/span><\/li>\n<li>Utilize user input to make decisions in a program<\/li>\n<li>Allow user input to change program flow during flight<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>Steps to Success:<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Input<\/li>\n<li>Getting the Info<\/li>\n<li>Dizzy Drone Activity<\/li>\n<li>Creating a Menu<\/li>\n<li>Make Your Own Menu<\/li>\n<li>Resetting and User Input<\/li>\n<li>Making Decisions<\/li>\n<li>Final Code<\/li>\n<li>Challenge: Custom Polygon<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22147a397ea\" name=\"6a22147a397ea\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 2<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Input<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Up to this point, when we hit that green play button, we have no control over the drone&#8217;s movements. In this lesson, we will learn how to interact with our drone and change it&#8217;s flight path while the program is running using the <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">input()<\/code> function.<\/p>\n<p>&nbsp;<\/p>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22147a39901\" name=\"6a22147a39901\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 3<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Getting the Info<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]So how can we get information to the drone mid-flight, or just after the program has started running? Just like sensors, we can send the drone values while it is operating! Python has a convenient command for this called INPUT.<\/p>\n<p>The INPUT function is actually pretty simple, it looks like this:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">stored_value = input(\"Enter a value to save\")<\/pre>\n<p>What you will notice is that we always save the input to a variable. The only parameter it has is a string that it uses to prompt the user to enter a question. To see this in action, watch the video below and follow along:<\/p>\n<div style=\"width: 1200px;\" class=\"wp-video\"><video class=\"wp-video-shortcode\" id=\"video-3722-1\" width=\"1200\" height=\"675\" preload=\"metadata\" controls=\"controls\"><source type=\"video\/mp4\" src=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2021\/09\/user-input.mp4?_=1\" \/><a href=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2021\/09\/user-input.mp4\">https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2021\/09\/user-input.mp4<\/a><\/video><\/div>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22147a39a03\" name=\"6a22147a39a03\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 4<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Dizzy Drone Activity<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Before we get started with the dizzy drone activity, we need to understand a little more about how the INPUT function works and what type of value it is actually giving us.<\/p>\n<div style=\"width: 1200px;\" class=\"wp-video\"><video class=\"wp-video-shortcode\" id=\"video-3722-2\" width=\"1200\" height=\"675\" preload=\"metadata\" controls=\"controls\"><source type=\"video\/mp4\" src=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2021\/09\/string-to-int.mp4?_=2\" \/><a href=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2021\/09\/string-to-int.mp4\">https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2021\/09\/string-to-int.mp4<\/a><\/video><\/div>\n<p>&nbsp;<\/p>\n<p>To recap:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>The input function gives us a string.<\/li>\n<li>If we want to change the string to an integer, we use the <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">int(value)<\/code> function<\/li>\n<li>We can tell what data type a variable is by using the <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">type(value)<\/code> function<\/li>\n<li>To make our program more user-friendly, we need to make sure the prompt asks for the correct types of information<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text][vc_column_text]Now that we have an understanding of how to use the INPUT function, let&#8217;s try an example. For this activity, you will need to do the following:<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Import CoDrone_mini<\/li>\n<li>Import Time<\/li>\n<li>Create and Pair your drone object<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>Once you have done this, we want to write a program that tells the drone to spin around a set number of times. The number of times it will spin around is based on user input from the INPUT function.<\/p>\n<p>The code should look like:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">import time\r\n\r\nimport CoDrone_mini\r\nfrom CoDrone_mini import Sequence\r\ndrone = CoDrone_mini.CoDrone()\r\ndrone.pair()\r\n\r\n#Ask User how many times to spin around\r\nnum_spins = int(input(\"How many times should the drone spin:\"))\r\n\r\ndrone.takeoff()\r\ntime.sleep(4) #Give the drone time to finish takeoff routine\r\n\r\ndrone.set_yaw(50)\r\ni=0\r\nwhile i &lt; num_spins: #start counting at 0, so less than\r\n    drone.move(5)\r\n    i += 1\r\ndrone.set_yaw(0) #reset the yaw before landing\r\ndrone.land()\r\ndrone.close()<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22147a39a8a\" name=\"6a22147a39a8a\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 5<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Creating a Menu<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400\">One of the best ways to see conditionals in action is by creating a menu of flight movements. From there, a user can enter a number using the \u00a0<code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">input()<\/code>\u00a0<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">function. The drone will then fly based on the code for a certain number. For example, after the number 3 is entered, the drone will fly in a triangle.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">This is one example of a menu. After a command is run, the menu will display again until \u201cExit\u201d is selected:<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">Menu:\r\n\r\nSpin in place\u00a0\r\nFly in a right angle\r\nFly in a triangle\r\nFly in a square\r\nExit<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22147a39b00\" name=\"6a22147a39b00\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 6<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Make Your Own Menu<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Ready to make your own? Import the CoDrone Mini, direction, sequence, and time libraries, and then create the drone object and pair your drone. Next, set up a while loop so that while true, the menu will be printed.\u00a0<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Inside the while loop, include print commands for a \u201cMenu\u201d and then options for different maneuvers that you would like your CoDrone Mini to do. This can be anything, but you should have an idea of how to program each maneuver you include! Make sure one of those options is \u201cQuit\u201d, which will end the while loop.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Your menu should look something like this:<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">import CoDrone_mini\r\n\r\nfrom CoDrone_mini import Direction\r\n\r\nfrom CoDrone_mini import Sequence\r\n\r\nimport time\r\n\r\ndrone = CoDrone_mini.CoDrone()\r\n\r\ndrone.pair()\r\n\r\n\r\nwhile True:\r\n\r\n    print(\"Menu: \")\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 print(\"1. Spin Around \\n\"\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0    \"2. Flip\\n\"\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\"3. Sway\\n\"\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\"4. Square\\n\"\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\"5. Quit\\n\")<\/pre>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Wondering what the \u00a0<\/span><code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">\\n<\/code> <span style=\"font-weight: 400\">is for? Without this, the menu could print very differently, like in one big line of code instead of in multiple statements in a block. Remember to line up the following lines at the next indent as shown so you don&#8217;t get an indentation error!<\/span><\/p>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22147a39b71\" name=\"6a22147a39b71\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 7<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Resetting and User Input<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Your program needs to be able to read user input and determine which set of code to execute. You will use if and else if statements to check if the user input is 1,2,3,4 or 5<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Make sure you\u2019re still in the while loop, and then set your drone\u2019s roll, pitch, yaw, and throttle to 0. That way, they will all be ready for each maneuver you program! Next, create a variable called <\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">option<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400\"> to store <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">input()<\/code><\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">. In the parameters for <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">input()<\/code><\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">, <\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">write<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400\"> &#8220;Select an option: &#8220;. <\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">This will print the command, and when the user inputs a number, it will be stored as an option and called later on.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">This is what you should have now:<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">drone.set_throttle(0)\r\n\r\n    drone.set_roll(0)\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.set_yaw(0)\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.set_pitch(0)\r\n\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 option = int(input(\"Select an option: \"))<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22147a39c61\" name=\"6a22147a39c61\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 8<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Making Decisions<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Our menu options will take advantage of what are called Sequences. To learn more about Sequences and how they can be used in our program, watch the video below:<\/p>\n<div style=\"width: 1200px;\" class=\"wp-video\"><video class=\"wp-video-shortcode\" id=\"video-3722-3\" width=\"1200\" height=\"675\" preload=\"metadata\" controls=\"controls\"><source type=\"video\/mp4\" src=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2021\/09\/sequences.mp4?_=3\" \/><a href=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2021\/09\/sequences.mp4\">https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2021\/09\/sequences.mp4<\/a><\/video><\/div>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text][vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Depending on the user input, your CoDrone will fly a different pattern! The best way to set this up is with conditionals. Have your drone take off, and then for option 1, set up an if statement and include your drone\u2019s maneuver.\u00a0<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">drone.takeoff()\r\n\r\n    if option == 1:\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0    drone.set_yaw(30)\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.move(2)<\/pre>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">For options 2 through 4, use else if statements, and then include drone maneuvers.<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">    elif option == 2:\r\n        drone.flip(Direction.FORWARD)\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\r\n\r\n    elif option == 3:\r\n\r\n        drone.fly_sequence(Sequence.SWAY)\r\n\r\n    elif option == 4:\r\n\r\n        drone.fly_sequence(Sequence.SQUARE)<\/pre>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">For option 5, use another else if statement again, but include a print command that says, \u201cYou have quit the menu\u201d and then a break. This will exit the loop.<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">    elif option == 5:\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0    print(\"You have quit the menu.\")\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 break<\/pre>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Next, include an else statement that will work when an invalid user input (like a number outside of 1-5) is entered.<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">    else:\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0    print(\"Not a valid input. Try again.\")<\/pre>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Finally, have your drone land and then (this is optional) include a print command that says \u201cDone.\u201d<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">    drone.land()\r\n\r\nprint(\"Done\")<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22147a39cc6\" name=\"6a22147a39cc6\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 9<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Final Code<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Your final program should look something like this:<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">import CoDrone_mini\r\n\r\nfrom CoDrone_mini import Direction\r\n\r\nfrom CoDrone_mini import Sequence\r\n\r\nimport time\r\n\r\ndrone = CoDrone_mini.CoDrone()\r\n\r\ndrone.pair()\r\n\r\n\r\nwhile True:\r\n\r\n    print(\"Menu: \")\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 print(\"1. Spin Around \\n\"\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0    \"2. Flip\\n\"\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\"3. Sway\\n\"\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\"4. Square\\n\"\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\"5. Quit\\n\")\r\n\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.set_throttle(0)\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.set_roll(0)\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.set_yaw(0)\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.set_pitch(0)\r\n\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 option = input(\"Select an option: \")\r\n\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.takeoff()\r\n\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 if option == \"1\":\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0    drone.set_yaw(30)\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.move(2)\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 elif option == \"2\":\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.flip(Direction.FORWARD)\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 elif option == \"3\":\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0    drone.fly_sequence(Sequence.SWAY)\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 elif option == \"4\":\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.fly_sequence(Sequence.SQUARE)\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 elif option == \"5\":\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 print(\"You have quit the menu.\")\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 break\r\n\r\n    else:\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 print(\"Not a valid input. Try again.\")\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.land()\r\n\r\nprint(\"Done\")\r\ndrone.close()<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22147a39d3d\" name=\"6a22147a39d3d\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 10<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Challenge: Custom Polygon<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]For this challenge, you will need to create a program that does the following:<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Takes in a value between 2 and 6 (2 is a circle)<\/li>\n<li>Prints out a menu with each value option<\/li>\n<li>Uses that value to make the appropriate sided polygon (for example, 4 would make the drone fly in a square)<\/li>\n<li>Takeoff before requesting user input<\/li>\n<li>Land once the polygon is completed.<\/li>\n<li>Use at least one Sequence in the program.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>It is strongly recommended that you go back to the for loops lesson and use your polygon program that you have already written and modify it to fit these new requirements. (We call this\u00a0<strong>code reuse<\/strong>, and it is a popular concept in the professional world of programming)<\/p>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row]<\/p>\n<\/div>","protected":false},"excerpt":{"rendered":"[vc_row][vc_column] Step 11 Goals and Steps to Success [vc_column_text]Goals: By the end of this lesson, I will be able to: Identify the importance of input in programs Create and run&nbsp;<a href=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/2-6-user-input\/\">&hellip;<\/a>","protected":false},"featured_media":4150,"template":"","meta":{"_initial_content":"","_new_post":false,"_quiz_has_questions":false,"_lesson_complexity":"","_lesson_length":10,"_lesson_course":3473,"_lesson_preview":"preview"},"lesson-category":[1782,1848,1793,1826,1482,853,694,59,171,63],"lesson-tag":[2135,2125,2119,2121,2122,2134,2111,2136],"class_list":["post-3722","lesson","type-lesson","status-publish","has-post-thumbnail","hentry","lesson-tag-beginner","lesson-tag-codrone-mini","lesson-tag-conditionals","lesson-tag-keyboard","lesson-tag-programming","lesson-tag-python","lesson-tag-remote-control","lesson-tag-user-input","module-level-2","post","odd"],"_links":{"self":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/3722","targetHints":{"allow":["GET"]}}],"collection":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons"}],"about":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/types\/lesson"}],"version-history":[{"count":10,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/3722\/revisions"}],"predecessor-version":[{"id":4492,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/3722\/revisions\/4492"}],"wp:featuredmedia":[{"embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media\/4150"}],"wp:attachment":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media?parent=3722"}],"wp:term":[{"taxonomy":"lesson-category","embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lesson-category?post=3722"},{"taxonomy":"lesson-tag","embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lesson-tag?post=3722"}],"curies":[{"name":"wp","href":"https:\/\/api.w.org\/{rel}","templated":true}]}}